  <section id="about" class="about" aria-labelledby="about-heading">
    <h2 id="about-heading">About Us</h2>
    <p style="text-align:justify">
    Brigadier General Evans Fordyce Carslon was a legendary United States Marine Corps officer, who served as a military observer in China. An admirer of Mao Zedong’s forces, Carlson modeled his elite unit during the Second World War, the 2nd Marine Raider Battalion, off of them, going so far as to co-opt their “gung ho” phrase, pioneering special operations in the process. Following the war, Carlson was outspokenly opposed to continued United States military and economic aid to Chiang Kai-shek’s forces, who were now back to fighting Mao’s forces following the defeat of the Empire of Japan. Carlson’s advocacy led him alongside entertainer Paul Robeson to co-chair the National Committee to Win the Peace, an organization that advocated for the withdrawal of American and British forces from China, nuclear disarmament, and peace with the Soviet Union. This anti-imperialist  advocacy caused Carslon to be blacklisted by the House Un-American Activities Committee, all for doing his patriotic duty and advocating for the original vision for America as an anti-imperialist project. The Evans Carlson Society follows in the footsteps of its namesake by calling upon its members to do their patriotic duty by taking up the cause of opposing American imperialism through the various activities of the organization.
    </p>
